5
# ir_diff: programs where the x86 emitter and the x86-via-IR path disagree, held so the count cannot
# grow. Measured on a clean checkout of upstream main 950a1c41, corpus 2331 programs
# (with-ir=638, ir-fns=1129, agree=633). Each row below is named in the gate output; four of the
# five are ONE class, and naming it is what makes the fifth worth reading on its own.
#
# CLASS ACCEPT-WHEN-EMITTER-REFUSES (4 rows) — x86 refuses the program (build 1) and the shared-IR
# path accepts it and runs, answering a value. A rejection the emitter enforces and the IR path does
# not is unsoundness in the IR path: the program reaches a binary. These are not "the IR path is
# more permissive, fine" — for reject_local_name_two_aggregate_types.al the file's own header says
# the program is VALID per Declarations §5/§6.1 and that the other three backends answer 109, so the
# test encodes x86's refusal as expected while the spec says the run should succeed. Either the
# refusal is the defect and the test is a workaround, or the IR path is wrong to run it; that is a
# decision to take in the spec, not to guess, and it is why these rows are held rather than waived.
#   test/deep_field_agg_enum_reject.al        x86 build 1   x86-ir run 42
#   test/deep_field_agg_var_reject.al         x86 build 1   x86-ir run 42
#   test/packed_in_plain_struct.al            x86 build 1   x86-ir run 20
#   test/reject_local_name_two_aggregate_types.al  x86 build 1   x86-ir run 109
#
# WRONG EXIT CODE (1 row) — both paths build and run, and answer differently: 25 against 132. This
# is a value the program gets wrong on one of the two paths, and the file names the mechanism (a
# negated operand), the same family as the `neg` mnemonic that exists on x86_64 and i386 alone.
#   test/lower_bugA_neg_operand.al            x86 run 25    x86-ir run 132
#
# Lowering the number means fixing a row, not editing this file: a row leaves by being fixed, and
# the class it belonged to should be gone with it.
